---
// The DevOps skill's showcase. Deliberately the only diagram on the page with
// this silhouette: Cloud converges (many decisions into one platform), AI runs
// a snake, QA runs a loop. DevOps is the exact inverse of Cloud — one artifact
// out to many destinations — so it fans. Same visual vocabulary as Cloud,
// opposite direction, which makes the two read as a deliberate pair rather than
// two attempts at the same picture. CloudTargetsDiagram jumps out to
// `#card-devops`; the two links above step 1 close that loop from this side.
//
// Hotspots map directly to the step ids in src/content/cards/devops.md. Keep
// them in lockstep or the click-to-reveal wiring silently does nothing.
//
// The geometry is the argument, not decoration:
//   · step 2 is drawn as the trunk every branch leaves from, because that is
//     literally what shared composite actions are — one body of pipeline code
//     serving five destinations.
//   · step 4 is the five destination chips themselves. All five carry
//     data-detail="artifact", so clicking any one lights all five: verification
//     is one step with five endpoints, not five steps.
//   · the dashed vertical is the clearance gate. Three branches cross it; the
//     two development branches pass below where it ends. That makes the card's
//     headline readable as geometry, with no caption doing the work.
//   · the low sweep back into step 4 is the drain: five out, one back. Not a
//     loop — QA owns the loop, and operations isn't a return to planning.
//
// Monitoring is planned, and it isn't built yet — `details[].planned: true` in
// devops.md marks it, and this diagram matches that by graphing it greyed out
// (`.node.is-planned` / `.hotspot.is-planned`) rather than drawing it as a live
// workflow. Keep the two in sync: if the card stops calling it planned, the
// diagram should stop greying it out in the same commit.
//
// Five destinations, not a 3x3 grid: game server and web client each go to
// production and development, Android goes to one place (a Play Console
// internal testing track). A grid would force a fudged or empty Android cell;
// an uneven fan draws the real shape honestly.
//
// Box text stays plain-English, same rule as Cloud's diagram. Named
// disciplines live one layer down in `details[].keywords`; the specifics
// (hash verification, signing keys, store version resolution, Argo CD) in
// `details[].body` and the card's trailing note.
//
// Text budget: 230w ≈ 26 label chars / 33 sub chars; 190w ≈ 21 / 27;
// 225w chips ≈ 25 label chars; 340w ≈ 39 / 50.
// Source facts: src/content/cards/devops.md.
---

<figure class="card-diagram">
  <div class="card-diagram-scroll">
    <svg
      class="topology"
      viewBox="0 0 820 520"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="devops-pipeline-title devops-pipeline-desc"
      xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
      fill="none"
    >
      <title id="devops-pipeline-title">From code change to controlled release</title>
      <desc id="devops-pipeline-desc">
        One pipeline fanning out to five destinations, fed by the Cloud and AI
        cards. Step one, on the left: planning names the five destinations
        before anything is written — the game server and the web client each in
        production and in development, and the Android build in a limited Play
        Console internal testing track — and decides in the same breath how
        much trust each one gets. Step two is the trunk in the middle: a single
        body of shared pipeline code that every destination calls, so a fix
        lands once, with each destination still running its own steps where it
        actually differs, such as signing the Android build with a key
        unlocked only for that step, or hash-checking art before it is allowed
        into the build, either one failing the build outright. Step three is
        the five destinations on the right, reached by five branches leaving
        that one trunk. A dashed vertical clearance gate sits between them: the
        two production branches and the Android branch cross it, while the two
        development branches pass below where it ends, because development is
        the only destination allowed to start a deploy from a push, and each
        environment carries its own credentials so a leaked development key
        cannot reach production. Step four, at the bottom left and drawn
        greyed out, is monitoring: reading what is actually running in each
        environment and alerting on drift. It is on the roadmap, not built
        yet.
      </desc>

      <defs>
        <marker id="devops-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path class="arrow-head" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" stroke="none" />
        </marker>
        <marker id="devops-arrow-planned" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path class="arrow-head is-planned" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" stroke="none" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- inbound: this card is the other two combined, so it says so and links
           back rather than pretending the pipeline starts from nothing -->
      <a class="card-jump" href="#card-cloud" aria-label="Back to the Cloud card, where the environments and guardrails are decided">
        <text class="wire accent" x="15" y="86">environments and guardrails from Cloud</text>
      </a>
      <a class="card-jump" href="#card-ai" aria-label="Back to the AI card, where the brief and the review loop come from">
        <text class="wire" x="15" y="102">the brief and the review loop from AI</text>
      </a>
      <path class="link" d="M100,110 L100,137" marker-end="url(#devops-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="destination" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 1, choose the destination — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="137" width="230" height="104" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="178">1 · Choose the destination</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="200">five destinations, one pipeline</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M245,189 L285,189" marker-end="url(#devops-arrow)" />

      <!-- step 2 as the trunk: one body of code, five branches leave from it -->
      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="checks" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 2, run the checks — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="285" y="58" width="190" height="262" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="299" y="182">2 · Run the checks</text>
        <text class="sub" x="299" y="204">shared actions, tests and validation</text>
      </g>

      <text class="wire accent" x="390" y="26" data-detail="authenticate" role="button" tabindex="0">3 · Authenticate securely</text>
      <text class="wire" x="390" y="42" data-detail="authenticate" role="button" tabindex="0">OIDC access expires with the job</text>

      <!-- the gate stops short on purpose: development routes under its end -->
      <path class="link" d="M550,52 L550,214" stroke-dasharray="5 5" />
      <text class="wire" x="478" y="222">development passes below</text>

      <text class="label" x="580" y="44">4 · Verify the artifact</text>

      <path class="link" d="M475,189 C515,189 545,78 580,78" marker-end="url(#devops-arrow)" />
      <path class="link" d="M475,189 C515,189 545,130 580,130" marker-end="url(#devops-arrow)" />
      <path class="link" d="M475,189 C515,189 545,182 580,182" marker-end="url(#devops-arrow)" />
      <path class="link" d="M475,189 C515,189 545,248 580,248" marker-end="url(#devops-arrow)" />
      <path class="link" d="M475,189 C515,189 545,300 580,300" marker-end="url(#devops-arrow)" />

      <!-- all five chips share data-detail="artifact": one step, five endpoints -->
      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="artifact" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 4, verify the game server artifact — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="580" y="58" width="225" height="40" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="594" y="84">Game server · production</text>
      </g>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="artifact" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 4, verify the web client artifact — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="580" y="110" width="225" height="40" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="594" y="136">Web client · production</text>
      </g>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="artifact" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 4, verify the Android artifact — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="580" y="162" width="225" height="40" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="594" y="188">Android · internal track</text>
      </g>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="artifact" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 4, verify the development artifact — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="580" y="228" width="225" height="40" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="594" y="254">Game server · development</text>
      </g>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="artifact" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 4, verify the development artifact — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="580" y="280" width="225" height="40" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="594" y="306">Web client · development</text>
      </g>

      <!-- the drain: five out, one back. Not a loop — monitoring doesn't
           return to planning, it would read the destinations that are live.
           Dashed and faint because the thing it feeds is planned, not built. -->
      <path class="link is-planned" d="M692,320 C692,392 520,412 355,412" stroke-dasharray="4 5" marker-end="url(#devops-arrow-planned)" />
      <text class="wire" x="380" y="352">would read from the live destinations</text>

      <g class="hotspot is-planned" data-detail="monitoring" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Monitoring status, planned but not yet built — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="360" width="340" height="104" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="405">Monitoring status</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="427">planned — not built yet</text>
      </g>

      <text class="wire accent" x="15" y="492" data-detail="gates" role="button" tabindex="0">5 · Apply deployment gates · 6 · Release or clean up</text>
      <text class="wire" x="15" y="508" data-detail="release" role="button" tabindex="0">everything else clears a gate first; temporary infrastructure is removed when it is no longer needed</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="card-diagram-caption">
    From code change to controlled release · Click any step to read what it means.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
